Experiment: Polynomial ADT using Singly Linked List — Addition and Definite Integration
Course: Data Structures / Object-Oriented Programming with C
Files to be used/modified: term.h, term.c, poly.h, poly.c, client.c

Preamble
A polynomial is represented as a singly linked list of terms, where each term is a node holding a coefficient and an exponent:
typedef struct term {
&nbsp; &nbsp; double coeff;
&nbsp; &nbsp; int    exp;
&nbsp; &nbsp; struct term *next;
} term_t;
A polynomial is a pointer to the head node of such a list, with terms stored in strictly decreasing order of exponent. You are provided with (or expected to already have, from the previous lab) the basic scaffolding for term.h/term.c (node creation, printing a single term) and poly.h/poly.c (creating a polynomial from user input, displaying a polynomial, freeing a polynomial).
Two independent problems are to be solved in this experiment.

Part A — Addition of Two Polynomials
Problem Statement
Write a C program, organized using the term/poly/client module structure described above, that reads two polynomials from the user, adds them, and displays the resulting polynomial, without modifying the two input polynomials.
Specification
Step 1 — term.h / term.c
Ensure the term_t structure and the following utility functions exist:
term_t* create_term(double coeff, int exp);
void    print_term(term_t* ptr_term, int is_first);
Step 2 — poly.h / poly.c
Implement:
term_t* create_poly(void);                                  // reads terms from user, builds list
void    display_poly(term_t* ptr_poly);                      // prints polynomial in standard form
term_t* add_poly(term_t* ptr_poly1, term_t* ptr_poly2);       // returns a NEW polynomial = poly1 + poly2
void    destroy_poly(term_t* ptr_poly);                      // frees all nodes
add_poly must:
●	Traverse both input lists using two pointers, comparing exponents.
●	If exponents are equal, add the coefficients; include the term in the result only if the resulting coefficient is non-zero.
●	If exponents differ, copy the term with the larger exponent into the result and advance only that pointer.
●	Continue until both lists are exhausted, then append any remaining terms from whichever list is not yet empty.
●	Must not mutate ptr_poly1 or ptr_poly2.
Step 3 — client.c
int main(void) {
&nbsp; &nbsp; term_t *poly1, *poly2, *sum;
&nbsp;
&nbsp; &nbsp; printf("Enter first polynomial:\n");
&nbsp; &nbsp; poly1 = create_poly();
&nbsp;
&nbsp; &nbsp; printf("Enter second polynomial:\n");
&nbsp; &nbsp; poly2 = create_poly();
&nbsp;
&nbsp; &nbsp; sum = add_poly(poly1, poly2);
&nbsp;
&nbsp; &nbsp; printf("Polynomial 1: "); display_poly(poly1);
&nbsp; &nbsp; printf("Polynomial 2: "); display_poly(poly2);
&nbsp; &nbsp; printf("Sum         : "); display_poly(sum);
&nbsp;
&nbsp; &nbsp; destroy_poly(poly1);
&nbsp; &nbsp; destroy_poly(poly2);
&nbsp; &nbsp; destroy_poly(sum);
&nbsp; &nbsp; return 0;
}
Sample Input/Output
Enter first polynomial:
Number of terms: 3
Coefficient Exponent: 5 3
Coefficient Exponent: -2 2
Coefficient Exponent: 7 0
&nbsp;
Enter second polynomial:
Number of terms: 2
Coefficient Exponent: 3 2
Coefficient Exponent: 4 1
&nbsp;
Polynomial 1: 5x^3 - 2x^2 + 7
Polynomial 2: 3x^2 + 4x
Sum         : 5x^3 + x^2 + 4x + 7
Expected Learning Outcome
Merging two sorted linked lists with a custom combining rule, and dynamic memory management without a leak.

Part B — Definite Integral of a Polynomial
Problem Statement
Extend the same ADT so that, given a polynomial p(x) and two limits a and b, the program computes and prints the definite integral ∫ₐᵇ p(x) dx by first constructing the indefinite integral polynomial (without the constant of integration) and then evaluating it at the two limits.
Specification
Step 1 — term.h / term.c: widen the coefficient type
Change every occurrence of the coefficient's type from int to double, in both the structure and every function signature/implementation that uses it (create_term, print_term, etc.). This is required because integrating a term divides its coefficient by (exp + 1), which is not in general an integer.
Step 2 — term.h / term.c: term-level integration
Add the function:
void integrate_term(term_t* ptr_term, term_t* ptr_integrated_term);
Given an input term c·xⁿ, this function must write the integrated term (c / (n+1))·x^(n+1) into *ptr_integrated_term (i.e. it populates the fields of an already-allocated/passed-in node — it does not itself return a pointer). Handle input validation as appropriate (e.g. what should happen if n = -1?). Discuss this in your submission, even if you assume it will not occur in the test data.
Step 3 — poly.h / poly.c: polynomial-level integration and evaluation
●	Update the polynomial evaluation function so it returns a double instead of an int:
double eval_poly(term_t* ptr_poly, double x);
●	Add a new function that builds the integrated polynomial term-by-term using integrate_term, without adding a constant term:
term_t* integrate_poly(term_t* ptr_poly);
This must return a brand-new list; the original polynomial must remain unmodified so it can still be displayed/reused.
Step 4 — client.c: put it together
int main(void) {
&nbsp; &nbsp; term_t *poly, *integrated_poly;
&nbsp; &nbsp; double lower_limit, upper_limit;
&nbsp; &nbsp; double result;
&nbsp;
&nbsp; &nbsp; printf("Enter the polynomial to integrate:\n");
&nbsp; &nbsp; poly = create_poly();
&nbsp;
&nbsp; &nbsp; integrated_poly = integrate_poly(poly);   // no constant of integration
&nbsp;
&nbsp; &nbsp; printf("Enter lower limit: ");
&nbsp; &nbsp; scanf("%lf", &amp;lower_limit);
&nbsp; &nbsp; printf("Enter upper limit: ");
&nbsp; &nbsp; scanf("%lf", &amp;upper_limit);
&nbsp;
&nbsp; &nbsp; result = eval_poly(integrated_poly, upper_limit)
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;- eval_poly(integrated_poly, lower_limit);
&nbsp;
&nbsp; &nbsp; printf("Polynomial          : "); display_poly(poly);
&nbsp; &nbsp; printf("Integrated Polynomial: "); display_poly(integrated_poly);
&nbsp; &nbsp; printf("Definite integral from %.2lf to %.2lf = %.4lf\n",
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;lower_limit, upper_limit, result);
&nbsp;
&nbsp; &nbsp; destroy_poly(poly);
&nbsp; &nbsp; destroy_poly(integrated_poly);
&nbsp; &nbsp; return 0;
}
Sample Input/Output
Enter the polynomial to integrate:
Number of terms: 2
Coefficient Exponent: 3 2
Coefficient Exponent: 2 1
&nbsp;
Enter lower limit: 0
Enter upper limit: 2
&nbsp;
Polynomial           : 3x^2 + 2x
Integrated Polynomial: x^3 + x^2
Definite integral from 0.00 to 2.00 = 12.0000
(Check: ∫(3x² + 2x)dx = x³ + x² ⇒ at x=2: 8+4=12; at x=0: 0. Result = 12.)
Expected Learning Outcome
●	Propagating a type change (int → double) consistently across an entire module interface.
●	Separating term-level transformation logic from polynomial-level (list traversal) logic.
●	Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus using a data structure built for a different original purpose (addition), demonstrating ADT reusability.

