abular-nums">59%</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" data-icon-shape="non-circular" focusable="false" class="icon-sm" aria-hidden="true"><use href="/cdn/assets/sprites-shell-097001e7.svg#chevron-down-sm" fill="currentColor"></use></svg></button><div class="flex shrink-0 items-center gap-1"><button type="button" aria-label="Download" class="interactive-button flex h-9 w-9 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-lg border-0 bg-transparent p-0 hover:bg-token-surface-hover keyboard-focused:bg-token-surface-hover dark:hover:bg-token-main-surface-tertiary" data-state="closed"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" aria-hidden="true" class="icon"><use href="/cdn/assets/sprites-core-ceb34a6c.svg#be92c3" fill="currentColor"></use></svg></button><button type="button" aria-label="Exit full screen" class="interactive-button flex h-9 w-9 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-lg border-0 bg-transparent p-0 hover:bg-token-surface-hover keyboard-focused:bg-token-surface-hover dark:hover:bg-token-main-surface-tertiary" data-state="closed"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" aria-hidden="true" class="icon"><use href="/cdn/assets/sprites-core-ceb34a6c.svg#d019da" fill="currentColor"></use></svg></button></div></div></div></header><div aria-label="Polynomial_ADT_Laboratory_Experiment.docx" class="bg-token-bg-secondary h-full min-h-0 overflow-auto overscroll-contain" data-artifact-interaction-surface="docx" data-testid="docx-preview-panel" role="region" tabindex="0" style="--artifact-docx-preview-zoom: 0.59;"><div class="artifact-docx-preview-wrapper"><section class="artifact-docx-preview" style="padding: 50.4pt 57.6pt; width: 612pt; min-height: 792pt;"><article><p style="text-align: center;"><span style="font-weight: bold; min-height: 18pt; font-size: 18pt;">DATA STRUCTURES LABORATORY</span></p><p style="text-align: center;"><span style="font-weight: bold; min-height: 14pt; font-size: 14pt;">Experiment: Polynomial ADT Using Singly Linked List</span></p><p style="text-align: center;"><span style="font-style: italic;">Part A – Polynomial Addition  |  Part B – Definite Integration</span></p><p><span>Course: Data Structures / Object-Oriented Programming with C</span></p><p><span>Files to be used/modified: term.h, term.c, poly.h, poly.c, client.c</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_heading1"><span>Preamble</span></p><p><span>A polynomial is represented using a singly linked list of terms. Each node stores a coefficient, an exponent, and a pointer to the next node. Terms in the polynomial are stored in strictly decreasing order of exponent.</span></p><p><span>Basic term structure:</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Courier New&quot;; min-height: 9.5pt; font-size: 9.5pt;">typedef struct term {<br>    double coeff;<br>    int    exp;<br>    struct term *next;<br>} term_t;</span></p><p><span>For this experiment, term_t is the node type and a polynomial is represented by a polynomial ADT type (poly_t), whose internal representation is a pointer to the first term. The implementation should keep the ADT interface consistent across both parts.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_heading1"><span>Common ADT Requirements</span></p><p><span>Use the existing/basic scaffolding from the previous laboratory where applicable. The term module is responsible for term-level operations, while the polynomial module is responsible for list-level operations.</span></p><p><span>The following term-level functions must be available:</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Courier New&quot;;">term_t *create_term(double coeff, int exp);</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Courier New&quot;;">void print_term(term_t *ptr_term, int is_first);</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_heading1"><span>Part A – Addition of Two Polynomials</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_heading2"><span>Problem Statement</span></p><p><span>Write a modular C program that reads two polynomials, adds them using the polynomial ADT, and displays the original polynomials and their sum. The two input polynomials must not be modified during the addition operation.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_heading2"><span>Required Polynomial ADT Operations</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Courier New&quot;;">poly_t create_poly(void);</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Courier New&quot;;">void display_poly(poly_t poly);</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Courier New&quot;;">void add_poly(poly_t poly1, poly_t poly2, poly_t *sum);</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Courier New&quot;;">void destroy_poly(poly_t *poly);</span></p><p><span>The preferred interface is to pass the two input polynomials and a third polynomial as the output. Thus, add_poly must construct the result in the supplied output polynomial rather than returning a term_t pointer. The result must be a new polynomial and the input polynomials must remain unchanged.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_heading2"><span>add_poly Requirements</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>Traverse both input polynomials using two pointers.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>Compare the exponents of the current terms.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>If the exponents are equal, add their coefficients. Insert the resulting term only when the coefficient is non-zero.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>If the exponents differ, copy the term having the larger exponent into the result and advance only the corresponding input pointer.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>Continue until both input lists are exhausted.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>Append/copy all remaining terms from whichever input list is not yet exhausted.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>Do not modify either input polynomial.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>Allocate new nodes for the output polynomial and ensure all dynamically allocated memory is released.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_heading2"><span>Client Program Requirements</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Courier New&quot;; min-height: 9pt; font-size: 9pt;">int main(void) {<br>    poly_t poly1, poly2, sum;<br><br>    printf("Enter first polynomial:\n");<br>    poly1 = create_poly();<br><br>    printf("Enter second polynomial:\n");<br>    poly2 = create_poly();<br><br>    add_poly(poly1, poly2, &amp;sum);<br><br>    printf("Polynomial 1: "); display_poly(poly1);<br>    printf("Polynomial 2: "); display_poly(poly2);<br>    printf("Sum         : "); display_poly(sum);<br><br>    destroy_poly(&amp;poly1);<br>    destroy_poly(&amp;poly2);<br>    destroy_poly(&amp;sum);<br><br>    return 0;<br>}</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_heading2"><span>Sample Input / Output</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Courier New&quot;; min-height: 9pt; font-size: 9pt;">Enter first polynomial:<br>Number of terms: 3<br>Coefficient Exponent: 5 3<br>Coefficient Exponent: -2 2<br>Coefficient Exponent: 7 0<br><br>Enter second polynomial:<br>Number of terms: 2<br>Coefficient Exponent: 3 2<br>Coefficient Exponent: 4 1<br><br>Polynomial 1: 5x^3 - 2x^2 + 7<br>Polynomial 2: 3x^2 + 4x<br>Sum         : 5x^3 + x^2 + 4x + 7</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_heading2"><span>Expected Learning Outcome – Part A</span></p><p><span>Understand how to merge two sorted singly linked lists using a custom combining rule, create a new ADT value without modifying its inputs, and manage dynamically allocated memory without leaks.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_heading1"><span>Part B – Definite Integration of a Polynomial</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_heading2"><span>Problem Statement</span></p><p><span>Extend the polynomial ADT so that, for a given polynomial p(x) and lower and upper limits a and b, the program constructs the indefinite integral polynomial without a constant of integration and evaluates it at the two limits to compute the definite integral:</span></p><p style="text-align: center;"><span style="font-weight: bold;">∫ₐᵇ p(x) dx = F(b) − F(a)</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_heading2"><span>Step 1 – Term Module: Coefficient Type</span></p><p><span>The coefficient type must be double throughout term.h and term.c. Update the term structure and every related function signature and implementation that uses a coefficient. This is necessary because integration divides a coefficient by (exp + 1), which may produce a non-integer value.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_heading2"><span>Step 2 – Term-Level Integration</span></p><p><span>Add the following function:</span><span style="font-family: &quot;Courier New&quot;;"><br>void integrate_term(term_t *ptr_term, term_t *ptr_integrated_term);</span></p><p><span>For an input term c·xⁿ, integrate_term must populate an already allocated output node with (c/(n+1))·xⁿ⁺¹. The function does not allocate or return a node.</span></p><p><span>Input validation: the term x⁻¹ requires special handling because its integral is ln|x| and cannot be represented by the polynomial term structure. Decide and document the behavior for exp == -1 (for example, reject/report the term or state that the experiment assumes it will not occur in the input data).</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_heading2"><span>Step 3 – Polynomial-Level Integration and Evaluation</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Courier New&quot;;">double eval_poly(poly_t poly, double x);</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Courier New&quot;;">poly_t integrate_poly(poly_t poly);</span></p><p><span>integrate_poly must build and return a brand-new polynomial by traversing the input polynomial term-by-term and using integrate_term for each term. Do not add a constant of integration. The original polynomial must remain unchanged so that it can still be displayed or reused.</span></p><p><span>eval_poly must evaluate the polynomial at the supplied value of x and return a double.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_heading2"><span>Step 4 – Client Program Requirements</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Courier New&quot;; min-height: 9pt; font-size: 9pt;">int main(void) {<br>    poly_t poly, integrated_poly;<br>    double lower_limit, upper_limit;<br>    double result;<br><br>    printf("Enter the polynomial to integrate:\n");<br>    poly = create_poly();<br><br>    integrated_poly = integrate_poly(poly);<br><br>    printf("Enter lower limit: ");<br>    scanf("%lf", &amp;lower_limit);<br><br>    printf("Enter upper limit: ");<br>    scanf("%lf", &amp;upper_limit);<br><br>    result = eval_poly(integrated_poly, upper_limit)<br>           - eval_poly(integrated_poly, lower_limit);<br><br>    printf("Polynomial           : "); display_poly(poly);<br>    printf("Integrated Polynomial: "); display_poly(integrated_poly);<br>    printf("Definite integral from %.2lf to %.2lf = %.4lf\n",<br>           lower_limit, upper_limit, result);<br><br>    destroy_poly(&amp;poly);<br>    destroy_poly(&amp;integrated_poly);<br><br>    return 0;<br>}</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_heading2"><span>Sample Input / Output</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Courier New&quot;; min-height: 9pt; font-size: 9pt;">Enter the polynomial to integrate:<br>Number of terms: 2<br>Coefficient Exponent: 3 2<br>Coefficient Exponent: 2 1<br><br>Enter lower limit: 0<br>Enter upper limit: 2<br><br>Polynomial           : 3x^2 + 2x<br>Integrated Polynomial: x^3 + x^2<br>Definite integral from 0.00 to 2.00 = 12.0000<br><br>Check:<br>∫(3x² + 2x)dx = x³ + x²<br>F(2) = 8 + 4 = 12<br>F(0) = 0<br>Result = 12</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_heading2"><span>Expected Learning Outcome – Part B</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>Propagate a data-type change (int to double) consistently across a multi-file C ADT.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>Separate term-level transformation logic from polynomial-level linked-list traversal.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>Construct a new polynomial without modifying the source polynomial.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>Apply the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus using a linked-list polynomial ADT originally designed for polynomial operations.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_heading1"><span>Implementation Notes and Constraints</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>Maintain terms in strictly decreasing order of exponent.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>Use dynamic memory allocation for polynomial nodes and free every allocated node before program termination.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>The result of polynomial addition and the result of polynomial integration must be independent lists; neither operation may modify its input polynomial.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>Use double for all coefficient calculations and for polynomial evaluation.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>Handle allocation failures and invalid input appropriately where practical.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>Keep the implementation modular: term-level operations belong in term.c/term.h, polynomial-level operations belong in poly.c/poly.h, and user interaction belongs in client.c.</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_heading1"><span>Files to Submit</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>term.h</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>term.c</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>poly.h</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>poly.c</span></p><p class="artifact-docx-preview_listbullet artifact-docx-preview-num-1-0"><span>client.c</span></p></article><footer style="margin-bottom: calc(-19.2px); min-height: calc(19.2px);"><p class="artifact-docx-preview_footer" style="text-align: center;"><span style="min-height: 8pt; font-size: 8pt;">Data Structures Laboratory – Polynomial ADT</span></p></footer></section></div></div></section></div></div></div><div class="pointer-events-none fixed inset-0 z-[121]" data-artifact-preview-fullscreen-portal-layer="true"><div class="pointer-events-auto contents"></div></div></div><span data-radix-focus-guard="" tabindex="0" style="outline: none; opacity: 0; position: fixed; pointer-events: none;"></span></body></html>
